**Goal Model – Multi‑Robot Food Delivery & Dish Retrieval**

Below is a complete goal model for the described hospital‑room food‑delivery and dish‑retrieval mission.  
All goals are numbered depth‑first (G1 … G4.3).  
All tasks are numbered depth‑first (AT1 … AT14).  
Each goal and task contains a justification that explains why the chosen decomposition, runtime annotation and relation are appropriate for the mission description.

---

## 1. Goals

| **Name** | **Text** | **Runtime** | **Goal Type** | **Target / Enquired Info** | **Relation** | **Justification** |
|----------|----------|-------------|---------------|----------------------------|--------------|-------------------|
| **G1 – Deliver Food to Inpatient Room** | Deliver food from the kitchen to the inpatient’s room, either by placing it on the table or by having the patient/assistant retrieve it from the robot’s tray. | **FALLBACK(G2, G3)** | Achieve | Food delivered to the patient’s room (either on table or retrieved from tray). | OR | The mission can be satisfied by *either* table‑placement or tray‑retrieval. A fallback guarantees that if table‑placement fails, the system will try tray‑retrieval. |
| **G2 – Place Food on Table** | Place the meal on the inpatient’s room table. | **-** | Perform | – | – | Requires the robot’s special manipulation skill; a single action. |
| **G3 – Fetch Food from Tray** | Fetch a meal from the robot’s tray so the patient or an assistant can take it. | **;** | Achieve | Food fetched from tray and handed to a human. | AND | The fetch process is only meaningful after the system has queried the patient record (see G3.5). The sequential operator ensures the query happens first. |
| **G3.5 – Query Patient Record** | Query the patient record to determine whether the patient, a companion, or a nurse can retrieve food from the tray. | **-** | Query | Ability of patient/companion/nurse to retrieve from tray (yes/no). | – | The information is required before attempting any fetch; it is a pure information‑seeking goal. |
| **G3.6 – FetchFoodFromTrayFallback** | Attempt to fetch the food from the tray using the best available collaborator. | **FALLBACK(G3.1, FALLBACK(G3.2, FALLBACK(G3.3, G3.4)))** | Perform | – | OR | The mission may involve the patient, a companion, a nurse, or another robot. The fallback chain reflects the priority order and the uncertainty described in the mission. |
| **G3.1 – Patient Fetch Food** | Have the patient retrieve the meal from the robot’s tray. | **-** | Perform | – | – | The patient is the intended recipient when able. |
| **G3.2 – Companion Fetch Food** | Have a companion visitor retrieve the meal from the robot’s tray. | **-** | Perform | – | – | A companion may be present and able to fetch when the patient cannot. |
| **G3.3 – Nurse Fetch Food** | Have a nurse retrieve the meal from the robot’s tray. | **-** | Perform | – | – | A nurse may be available on‑call. |
| **G3.4 – Robot Fetch Food** | Robot autonomously retrieves the meal from its own tray. | **-** | Perform | – | – | The robot can fetch if no human is available. |
| **G4 – Retrieve Dishes** | Remove dirty dishes from the inpatient room. | **FALLBACK(G4.1, FALLBACK(G4.2, G4.3))** | Achieve | All dishes removed from the room. | OR | Dish retrieval may be unassisted, with robot‑robot cooperation, or with human cooperation. The fallback chain models the priority of the simplest method first. |
| **G4.1 – Retrieve Dishes Unassisted** | Robot removes dishes without any assistance. | **-** | Perform | – | – | Possible when the robot can reach and lift the dishes alone. |
| **G4.2 – Retrieve Dishes with Robot Cooperation** | Two robots cooperate to remove dishes. | **-** | Perform | – | – | Needed when a single robot cannot lift or reach the dishes. |
| **G4.3 – Retrieve Dishes with Human Cooperation** | Robot cooperates with a human to remove dishes. | **-** | Perform | – | – | Human assistance may be required for heavy or awkward dishes. |

